The '''Garrigàs Lake''' ({{Langx|ca|Llac Garrigàs}}), known as the '''Garrigàs Gulf''' ({{Lang-ca|Golfo garrigàs|links=no}}) prior to 1963, is a shallow [[W:Freshwater lake|freshwater lake]] of the [[Salsipuedes Sea]] in the northwest of the island of [[Atlántida]], extending about 100 km (60 miles) inland and at most 50 km (30 miles) wide, with an overall depth of about 4 to 5 metres (13–16 feet) and a coastline of about 470 km (292 miles). It covers almost {{Convert|5000|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}. 

Its name is Catalan for "barren land". In the early 1960s, the bay was cut off from the Salsipuedes Sea with the completion of the [[Mar Menor Works]]. The entirety of the salt water inlet gradually turned into a freshwater lake as a result of the rivers that drain into it. By means of drainage and [[W:Polders|polders]] and an area of some 1,500 km<sup>2</sup> (580 sq mi) was slated to be [[W:Land reclamation|reclaimed as land]]. Only about 20% of this reclamation came to fruition, as a contentious referendum in 1974 resulted in the revocation of cantonal approval for the project, and reclamation was subsequently halted.  

Due to considerable amounts of water from the [[Canyada (river)|Canyada]] flowing through its distributaries [[Akãjú]] and [[Amandá]] into the lake, it functions as a large [[W:Freshwater reservoir|freshwater reservoir]], serving as a source for agriculture and drinking water. Outlet sluices in the Garrigàs Seawall regulate the water level of the lake. It is used for transport and fishing. It also offers a number of opportunities for recreational activity, both on the water and on its shores. Due to the shallowness of the lake, its cities and fishing villages remained mostly unspoiled.  

